h just  picking apart what that means for our  bills and how much of our income we pay  on them and i can show you this line  goes all the way back to the 1950s the  blue area there is the kind of range the  share of our expenditure that's going on  our energy and you can see it's actually  been relatively low in recent decades  but look at what happens uh at the end  of this week uh the start of april when  that price cap uh comes in so that's  just under two thousand pounds highest  level uh since the mid-1980s as a result  of that um but the question now is what  happens  next and in october the obr the office  special responsibility they said last  week they did the official forecast for  the government they said potentially a  price cap of 2 800 pounds and just to  put that into perspective look at that  red line that is higher so the share of  what we're paying at the share of our  expenditure that's going on it the  higher than we've ever seen before the  70s but also the 60s also a lot of the  50s as well now bear in mind you've got  to factor in the fact that you've got  that 350 pounds in a council tax cut  rebates that are coming through from the  government so you bear that in mind but  even after you do that look that red  line still above those levels that we  peaks that we've seen going back to  1950s however as i showed you a moment  ago those prices are yoyoing around  quite a lot at the moment so  every single day that calculation  changes right now it's not looking like  2800 pounds it's looking more like this  is the latest forecast more like  2490 pounds so lower but still the red  line above anything we've paid uh before  as a share of our incomes but here again  you've got to do that calculation so  bear in mind what the government's doing  350 pounds take that off and we're down  to well it's not the highest level since  the mid 80s it's the highest level since  the early 80s even so we're talking  about historic uh thing going on here  maybe