ay isn't it inevitable that the  government's going to say let's increase  domestic north sea oil and gas  production at least in the short term to  replace  the russian oil and gas that we're we're  weaning ourselves off  yeah that is a big risk and it's you  know even a big risk that we might you  know decide to delay the closure of our  coal plants even you know in light of  this crisis but  i think actually more broadly um you  know this these terrible recent events  have prompted a major rethink in terms  of our you know over dependence really  the folly of over dependence on imported  gas both in terms of you know price  stability as well as energy security and  you know i think governments across  europe are actually beginning now to  look again at nuclear energy alongside  renewables and energy efficiency because  actually all of the evidence tells us  that although nuclear plants appear to  be quite slow  to be built once they are built they are  very very large so in terms of the  overall contribution that they make  towards adding  very reliable very stably priced and  very clean electricity generation to our  grids they're in fact the fastest way to  decarbonize our electric electricity  grids this has been demonstrated  previously in many countries including  sweden and france and i want to pick up  with nuclear again in a moment but first  just a quick thought again on domestic  oil and gas and michael jacobs i mean  the people who say do that now it's  going to be quicker and easier to do it  now to increase north sea oil production  we've got our own domestic supply that  way it's a short-term fix it's got kind  of gives us this base load of secure um  energy  into the mix where renewables have more  vagaries  well we can discuss uh the intermittency  question and the role of nuclear in a  second but in terms of north sea oil and  gas we need to be very careful about  saying that it's a short-term fix it  takes on average 28 years to move from  uh the beginning the exploration process  for new oil gas field and actually oil  and gas coming on stream now that's an  average but of course if it took 28  years that would take us to 2050 and  2050 is the year that we're meant to be  net zero so it's not a short-term  solution even if a new oil and gas field  could come on stream in 15 years but  this is still a very long time  and what we have is an immediate  requirement to move away from russian  oligarchs so it's not a short-term  fix that at all and of course it may  well be that because we are on a  generally declining trend of gas and oil  towards our net zero targets that the  private sector decides that it doesn't  want to invest in new fields because it  won't be able to sell all the oil and  gas  that will be found in them and they will  be left as so-called stranded assets  so i don't think new oil and gas fields  in the north sea is an answer to the  current uh questions that we face and  let's pick up on what kirsty was saying  that nuclear is the answer do you think  that's right i mean i know kirsten  you're i think advising the government  on this subject so you're you're a big  fan of it michael is nuclear that the  way forward  well the advantage of nuclear is that  its base load capacity um when the wind  uh doesn't blow so much and the sun  doesn't shine we need electricity that  is on all the time and  gas and coal-fired power stations have  done that for us in the past along with  nuclear and nuclear could do it in a low  carbon form in the future  the problem is the length of time it  takes to build new nuclear power  stations now  your report from tom earlier talked  about modular nuclear reactors which is  the new kind which are untested yet and  we don't know how long they could come  on stream but we do know that the  traditional large nuclear reactors take  a very long time to come on stream  hinckley point c  um is due to come on stream in 2026  having first been granted planning  permission in 2012. okay i'm going to  pick up a very long time to wait before  we will get that new capacity and in the  meantime new storage technologies for  electricity might well have proved  themselves a better option kirsten let's  pick up on those points then it just  takes way too long we haven't got time  have we with these net zero goals  looming  the thing is is that you know it it is  quite quick to build a solar project for  example but the solar project is a  thousand times smaller uh than a nuclear  plant and  then only has a ten percent capacity  factor which means it generates only ten  percent of its its name plate capacity  so that's why even though these big  nuclear plants do take a long time to  come on board once they're up and  running then they do represent a massive  quantity of electricity that we need but  i think i'd like to broaden this out a  little bit and remember that we're  actually you know only talking about 20  of our energy consumption right now  which is electricity actually what we we  also need to remember is that we have to  decarbonize the other 80  of our energy use which means liquid  fuels which means industry which means  heavy transport shipping aviation those  are really tough to decarbonize sectors  and one of the really big advantages for  nuclear technologies that i'm really  interested in is the fact that they can  produce high temperature heat now that's  something that wind and solar can't do  but it means that they can produce  very um of cost-effective hydrogen-based  clean synthetic fuels and heat for  industrial applications yeah and and  kirsty just one more thing on that very  high heat it's one of the reasons people  get nervous about it look at the  situation in ukraine people have  concerns about the chernobyl nuclear  plant for example people worry about  safety don't they and they worry about  nuclear waste as well so just touch on  those issues if you can  yeah i think we have to look at you know  any of these kind of risks and uh in  context and you know right now the  reality is is that we see seven million  premature deaths per year around the  world every single year from air  pollution so actually focusing on net  zero and cleaning up all of our you know  global fossil fuel-based energy  infrastructure is a really incredible  opportunity not only to you know address  the climate challenges that we face but  also the public health challenges that  we face and when you look at those risks  and those you know real material effects  that we're already experiencing today  before even the climate risks you start  to see that the risks presented by  nuclear energy are very well managed  okay and represent a much smaller risk  to public health in the environment and  finally very quickly just to put to both  of you  michael there's so many different  aspects to this whole issue but is there  a way of getting prices down for  consumers and it being good for the  planet  the best way of getting prices down for  consumers or getting bills down is to  invest in energy efficiency we waste so  much of our energy through our leaky  buildings  and our poor  energy efficiency and insulation  standards and that's not only the  cheapest method  of meeting our energy needs but is also  the thing that will most bring bills  down for  householders so i really want to see  that as the centerpiece of the  government's energy strategy whenever it  comes kirsty quick thought from you on  the same subject  yeah i absolutely agree energy  efficiency is critical we have to do all  of these things at once we have to move  forward in all fronts 28 years to  achieve net zero across our entire  fossil fuel energy system so we need a  very ambitious energy strategy from the  government which does include you know  investments in nuclear energy to deliver  you know cost-effective and very stable  and ultimately very secure reliable  energy  well when we do get that strategy it'd  be really interesting to get your  thoughts on there but for the moment uh  thanks very much indeed both of you for  joining us kester gogan michael jacobs  we appreciate your time thank you  well that's it from us for today but  joining me on the show tomorrow our  chief executive at forum for the future  dr sally uran and executive director of  climate outreach jamie clark thanks for  watching i'll see you next time  [Music]  [Music]  you

